Phase 9 proof run — preflight.sql and verify.sql exercised in BOTH directions
=============================================================================

WHEN      2026-09-16, this session.
WHERE     A throwaway loopback PostgreSQL cluster, 127.0.0.1:55439, database `phase9proof`.
          Created for this run, stopped at the end of it. No production credential was present
          in the environment (DATABASE_URL, DIRECT_URL and PROD_READONLY_URL all absent —
          checked before the cluster was started), so this was a development session, not a
          deploy session.

VERSION   PostgreSQL 16.13 (Ubuntu 16.13-0ubuntu0.24.04.1).
          *** DEGRADED. *** Production runs 17.6 and the phase prompt asks for 17.6. Only the
          PG16 server binaries are installed in this environment and the Docker daemon is not
          running, so 17.6 was unreachable. 16.13 is the same version phase 8's proof harness
          ran on (see prisma/drift-baseline.json, 2026-09-15). The objects exercised here —
          a btree unique index, a partial btree index, and an UPDATE — behave identically
          across 16 and 17, but that is a claim about Postgres, not something this run proved.
          CI's `migrations` job asserts its own major version and remains the authority.

NOTHING WAS RUN AGAINST PRODUCTION. The migration is AUTHORED, NOT APPLIED. Applying it is the
owner's, per run, under CLAUDE.md's production-database protocol.


THE FOUR RUNS
-------------

Chain built with `prisma migrate deploy` against the empty database: all 118 migration
directories applied, ending at 20261201000000_phase9_pickup_release_token.

PRE-STATE constructed to match the production pre-state the owner verified at 20:45 UTC:
  - the 20261201 ledger row deleted          -> 117 distinct applied names (production: 117)
  - pickups_token_hash_key dropped
  - pickups_live_release_token_idx dropped
  - ONE pickup seeded carrying plaintext in BOTH qr_code_data and qr_code_image
    (FK triggers disabled for the insert; a throwaway database, never production)

The seeded row is the point. `pickups` holds zero rows in production, and an assertion that
"no plaintext survives" is trivially true on an empty table — it passes while checking nothing,
which is the defect class this whole directory exists for. The row makes the assertion falsifiable.

  RUN 1  preflight.sql  on the PRE-state   -> 17 rows, ALL CHECKED, zero BLOCK.
                                              Terminal row: "17 assertions: 13 block-capable,
                                              3 reported" — matches the 17 rows emitted.
                                              Assertion 8 read "117 distinct / 117 rows /
                                              0 rolled back": the owner's pre-state, asserted
                                              rather than taken.
                                              Assertion 9 read "1 of 1 pickups" carrying
                                              plaintext — the scale of the irreversible UPDATE,
                                              known before it ran.

  RUN 2  verify.sql     on the PRE-state   -> 8 MISSING, 7 PRESENT, 1 terminal.
                                              MISSING: both indexes; the VALID+UNIQUE check;
                                              the partial index's VALID, predicate and key
                                              checks; "no plaintext release credential
                                              survives"; and both ledger rows.
                                              This is the run that proves verify.sql CAN fail.
                                              A verify that has only ever been run after a
                                              successful deploy has never been tested.

  -- `prisma migrate deploy` applied 20261201000000 --

  RUN 3  preflight.sql  on the POST-state  -> 4 BLOCK rows, correctly:
                                              both indexes ALREADY PRESENT;
                                              ledger ALREADY APPLIED;
                                              chain moved to 118 distinct, no longer the
                                              verified pre-state.
                                              This is the run that proves preflight.sql CAN
                                              stop a run.

  RUN 4  verify.sql     on the POST-state  -> 0 MISSING, 15 PRESENT, 1 terminal = 16 rows.
                                              Terminal row: "16 assertions: 12 physical,
                                              3 ledger." — matches the 16 rows emitted.

BACKFILL EVIDENCE (the reason run 2 seeded a row):

  before:  pf-1 | qr_code_data={"type":"autolenis_pickup","nonce":"k3j4h"} | qr_code_image=data:image/png;base64,AAAA
  after:   pf-1 | qr_code_data=NULL                                        | qr_code_image=NULL

  The row still exists; both credential columns are NULL. The UPDATE cleared real data rather
  than matching zero rows.

INDEXES AS ACTUALLY BUILT (read back from pg_indexes, not from the migration text):

  CREATE UNIQUE INDEX pickups_token_hash_key ON public.pickups USING btree (token_hash)
  CREATE INDEX pickups_live_release_token_idx ON public.pickups USING btree (token_expires_at)
    WHERE ((token_hash IS NOT NULL) AND (token_consumed_at IS NULL) AND (token_revoked_at IS NULL))

  The partial predicate is the resolver's WHERE clause in release-token.service.ts, which is
  what verify.sql assertion 5 checks clause by clause.


THE DRIFT GUARD — prisma/__tests__/phase9-proof-sql.test.ts
------------------------------------------------------------

10 tests, all passing. Exercised in both directions by temporary mutation, each reverted and
the restore confirmed clean (`git diff` = 0 lines):

  MUTATION                                              RESULT
  drop the migration from verify.sql's expected list -> 2 failures: "verify.sql does not name
                                                        20261201000000_..." and the phantom-name
                                                        check on the placeholder that replaced it
  shorten the prefix to 202612010\d{2} (phase 8's     -> 3 failures, caught by the anti-vacuity
    exact bug: two digits short of the 14-digit stamp)  assertion: "expected at least 1 phase 9
                                                        migration, found 0"
  make verify.sql's terminal row claim 11 physical    -> 1 failure: "terminal row claims 16
                                                        assertions (11 physical, 3 ledger) but
                                                        the file emits 16 rows (12 physical,
                                                        3 ledger, 1 terminal)"
  all reverted                                        -> 10 pass / 0 fail, git diff clean

ONE GUARD BUG FOUND AND FIXED BY THIS EXERCISE. The first draft refused the phrase
`/(one|two|three|four|five)\s+applied\s+rows?/` and fired on three CORRECT lines in verify.sql:
"one applied row PER phase 9 migration" is the per-migration idiom — it stays true at one
migration and at five, and the total beside it is derived from the CTE. Phase 8's guard
deliberately matched only the plural. The guard was wrong, not the file; the pattern now refuses
a plural total ("two applied rows") and permits the singular per-migration phrasing.


=============================================================================
SECOND PROOF RUN — 20261201000100_phase9_possession_condition added mid-phase
=============================================================================

WHEN      2026-09-17, this session.
WHERE     A second throwaway loopback cluster, 127.0.0.1:55440, database `phase9proof2`.
          Created for this run, stopped at the end of it (`pg_isready` refused afterwards).
          DATABASE_URL, DIRECT_URL and PROD_READONLY_URL were all checked absent BEFORE the
          cluster was started — `[ -n "$VAR" ]`, the only sanctioned presence check — so this
          was a development session, not a deploy session.

          THE DATA DIRECTORY IS NOT IN THE SESSION SCRATCHPAD, and that is not a preference.
          The scratchpad is mode 0700 root-owned; `initdb` refuses to run as root and the
          `postgres` user cannot traverse it. The cluster lives at /var/tmp/pg9b and is
          reclaimed with the container.

VERSION   PostgreSQL 16.13 — the SAME DEGRADATION as the first run, for the same reason (only
          the PG16 server binaries are installed; production runs 17.6). Unchanged claim: the
          objects exercised here behave identically across 16 and 17, but this run did not
          prove that. CI's `migrations` job remains the authority.

WHY A SECOND RUN AT ALL. Phase 9 acquired a second migration after the first proof run. §Stage
20's thirteenth precondition is "Buyer possession, VIN, mileage, and condition confirmed", and
implementing it surfaced that `recordDealerRelease` and `confirmPossession` were writing the
DEALERSHIP's and the BUYER's condition reports to the same column — so every completed handover
destroyed the dealership's Stage 18 record and relabelled the buyer's as the dealer's. The
repair is a column, and a column is a migration.

THE DRIFT GUARD CAUGHT THE OMISSION BEFORE THIS RUN DID. `phase9-proof-sql.test.ts` went red the
moment the migration directory appeared, on both "preflight.sql names every phase 9 migration on
disk" and the verify.sql equivalent — which is the entire reason it derives the expected set from
disk rather than trusting the prose. It then went red a SECOND time, on the recomputed terminal
row counts, because adding assertions changed how many rows each file emits and the summary lines
still claimed the old numbers. Neither would have been noticed by reading.

NOTHING WAS RUN AGAINST PRODUCTION. Both migrations are AUTHORED, NOT APPLIED. Applying them is
the owner's, per run, under CLAUDE.md's production-database protocol.


THE SIX RUNS
------------

Chain built with `prisma migrate deploy` against the empty database: all 119 migration
directories applied, ending at 20261201000100_phase9_possession_condition.

PRE-STATE reconstructed to match the production state the owner verified at 20:45 UTC:
  - both 20261201 ledger rows deleted        -> 117 distinct applied names (production: 117)
  - pickups_token_hash_key dropped
  - pickups_live_release_token_idx dropped
  - condition_at_possession dropped
  - ONE pickup seeded carrying plaintext in BOTH qr_code_data and qr_code_image

  RUN   WHAT                                    RESULT
  1     verify.sql on the APPLIED state         18 assertions PRESENT, 0 MISSING. The derived
                                                count printed "2 expected" without the number
                                                appearing anywhere in the file.
  2     preflight.sql on the PRE-STATE          19 rows CHECKED, 0 BLOCK. The plaintext row read
                                                "1 of 1 pickups" — non-empty, so falsifiable.
  3     verify.sql on the PRE-STATE             11 MISSING, across BOTH halves: six index
                                                assertions, the plaintext assertion, the new
                                                column, both ledger rows, and the derived total.
  4     preflight.sql on the APPLIED state      6 BLOCK: the new column "ALREADY PRESENT —
                                                created out of band?", both indexes, both ledger
                                                rows, and the chain reading 119 against 117.
  5     20261201000100 applied a SECOND time    NOTICE "column already exists, skipping"; verify
                                                still 0 MISSING. The IF NOT EXISTS idempotency
                                                claim is exercised, not asserted.
  6     rollback.sql, then verify               exactly one MISSING — pickups.condition_at_
                                                possession — and nothing else. Re-applying the
                                                migration returned verify to 0 MISSING.

BOTH FILES ARE NOW PROVEN TO FAIL as well as to pass, on both halves, with the second migration
included. Run 3 is verify.sql's failing direction; run 4 is preflight.sql's. Runs 5 and 6 are the
migration's own, which the first proof run did not cover for a column because there was no column.

WHAT THIS RUN DID NOT PROVE. The seeded pickup carries a plaintext credential but no release
token, so nothing here exercised the UNIQUE index against a real collision — the duplicate-hash
assertion read 0 of 0 and is, on this fixture, vacuous. It is falsifiable in principle (two rows
sharing a hash) and is NOT falsified here. NOT VERIFIED, stated rather than glossed.


=============================================================================
THIRD RUN — the duplicate-hash assertion, which was passing against nothing
=============================================================================

WHEN      2026-09-17, this session. Same throwaway loopback cluster, database `phase9dup`.
          DATABASE_URL / DIRECT_URL / PROD_READONLY_URL checked absent first.

WHY. The second run's own closing note said it: `preflight.sql`'s "duplicate token_hash values"
assertion read **0 of 0** on that fixture — falsifiable in principle, not falsified. The owner's
ruling, 2026-09-17: *"An assertion that passes against an empty set proves nothing. Either seed a
duplicate in the proof fixture so it fires, or state in the file that this assertion is
unexercised — but do not leave it looking like coverage."*

That is the EIGHTH instance of §8.1h's class, and it is in the file written to catch that class.
An assertion counted as coverage while checking nothing is the exact shape §8.1h catalogues; it
reached the proof directory itself. Seeded rather than disclaimed: a disclaimer says the guard is
untested, a seeded fixture says it works.

PRE-STATE. As the second run, plus **two pickups sharing one `token_hash`** (`pu_dup_a`,
`pu_dup_b`, both `repeat('a',64)`), the unique index dropped.

  WHAT                                              RESULT
  preflight on the duplicate                    ->  "duplicate token_hash values (what CREATE
                                                    UNIQUE INDEX fails on) | 1 | BLOCK"
                                                    The assertion FIRES.
  its sibling, also previously 0 of 0           ->  "pickups carrying a token_hash (first writer
                                                    ships with this migration) | 2 | BLOCK"
                                                    TWO vacuous assertions exercised, not one.
  20261201000000 applied WITH the duplicate     ->  REFUSED, exit 3:
                                                    ERROR: could not create unique index
                                                    "pickups_token_hash_key"
                                                    DETAIL: Key (token_hash)=(aaa...) is duplicated.
                                                    The assertion guards a REAL failure — the
                                                    migration would have died mid-chain.
  duplicate removed, preflight re-run           ->  "duplicate token_hash values | 0 | CHECKED"
  both seeded rows removed, migration applied   ->  0 ERROR lines; applies cleanly

BOTH DIRECTIONS, ON THE ASSERTION ITSELF. It blocks when a duplicate exists, clears when it does
not, and the thing it blocks is a migration that genuinely fails. Nothing here is a claim about
Postgres — the failure was produced and read.

WHAT IS STILL NOT EXERCISED, stated rather than glossed: `verify.sql`'s duplicate-hash assertion
(the POST-apply one) remains structurally unfalsifiable, and that is correct rather than a gap —
once `pickups_token_hash_key` exists the database itself refuses a duplicate, so no fixture can
produce one to detect. It is a belt-and-braces read of a constraint the schema enforces. The
PRE-apply assertion in `preflight.sql`, which runs in the window where a duplicate CAN exist, is
the one that needed proving, and now has it.
